# Ebb — Cold Start

State lives in git and the work graph, so this works after any shutdown, reboot, or fresh clone. The one thing written on the way out is the handoff note — see Shutdown below and `wheelhouse/runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md`'s "End of day".

## Minimum viable fleet

Commander + producer + one worker + one reviewer. Start there. The producer is required; this install runs it as a commander subagent with `wheelhouse/crew/PRODUCER.md`, not a separate standing process. The commander does not write intent or outcomes. It is a working loop, and it is what makes this installable rather than aspirational.

## 1. Commander

```bash
cd ~/code/ebb
claude
```

The folder's `CLAUDE.md` makes this session the commander. No launcher required. Wire Claude Code's Stop hook to `bash seats/principal-sentinel.sh` so a final `@principal:` block becomes a durable need instead of scrollback. Wire the sibling inbox hooks to `seats/inbox-hook.sh`: Dispatch Office records arrive at session start, with each prompt and after each shell command. The herald's poke is the prompt that carries them. Read every delivered record before the turn ends: human answers with needs `show`, signals through intake, relay requests through comms; inbound is information, not authority.

**If `wheelhouse/HANDOFF.md` exists, read it before anything else** — it is
yesterday's commander writing to you: which seats to `resume` versus respawn,
what is waiting on a verdict, the first dispatch to make. After ingesting it,
archive it to `wheelhouse/handoffs/<sign-off date>.md` per the runbook's "Next
morning" section. No note is the normal first-morning state — start from the
graph.

> If you run your own agent harness, launch the commander with it instead — the folder context does the rest.

## 2. Seats

Each seat is a persistent harness process the commander owns — provisioned once, then started with the adapter. The roster chooses the harness per seat (`pi`, `claude-code`, or `codex`); the same role brief is injected at spawn. Nothing here is a terminal you keep open, and there is nothing to paste into a seat. `wheelhouse/fleet/SEATS.md` is the contract; `seats/README.md` documents every command below.

Provision each roster seat once (safe to re-run; it changes nothing for a seat that exists). The script creates the seat's agent directory under the historical `$HOME/.pi-seats-ebb/` root and prints the one-time login for the harness and account that seat should BE — for example `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=... pi`, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=... claude auth login --claudeai`, or `CODEX_HOME=... codex login`, matching the roster. Run that login before the first spawn:

```bash
cd ~/code/ebb
seats/seat-env.sh ebb worker-1
seats/seat-env.sh ebb reviewer-1
seats/seat-env.sh ebb verifier
```

Then, on any later cold start, just spawn from the roster:

```bash
bun seats/adapter.ts spawn worker-1
bun seats/adapter.ts spawn reviewer
bun seats/adapter.ts status          # every standing seat, liveness
# no spawn for verifier: it is an ephemeral consumer-surface walker started by seats/walk.ts
```

After a bead-bound `spawn <seat> <bead-id>` or dispatch has prepared the worker's worktree (bare spawn starts at the install root), check its factory skills and project manual; a refusal stops this startup until the named skill or cwd is repaired:

```bash
bun seats/adapter.ts skills-check worker-1
```

`resume <seat>` instead of `spawn` reattaches a seat to the session it was stopped with, context warm — use it when a stop or reboot interrupted work in flight.

## 3. Watch it

The commander dispatches by name (`bun seats/adapter.ts dispatch <seat> <bead-id> ...`) and each seat's event stream lands in `seats/logs/<seat>.jsonl`. The bridge is the cockpit for watching the fleet: `seats/cockpit.sh ebb` builds (or re-attaches to — never duplicates, and re-runs respawn a missing floor pane) the tmux session `wh-ebb`, commander pane on the left and the floor — `bun seats/floor.ts`, read-only: spotlight one seat, rail of every seat's attention cues — on the right. The supervisor owns the Dispatch Office herald, the local desk, and the declared courier; cockpit verifies the supervisor and records the commander pane for herald self-aim in `seats/run/commander-pane.json`. Open the desk URL from `seats/run/desk.port` for `/needs` and the read-only `/board`; the floor is the commander's view and the board is the human's kanban. Later inbox traffic leaves `poke sent`, `poke cooldown`, or `poke not-idle` proof in `seats/logs/herald.out.log`. Keys and cue meanings are in `seats/README.md`'s "The bridge" section; `bun seats/adapter.ts status` is the no-cockpit fallback that always works, but it does not start the supervisor-owned daemons.

## 4. Feed it

Speak backlog items to the commander in plain language. It files them as described tasks in the graph; the fleet claims them; workers report done on the task record with comments and the `needs-review` label without closing; the reviewer gates branches with bench evidence; and only the commander/integrator closes after merge per this project's merge policy. Work decomposed from the ISA Goal lands on its goal branch and closes there; only the outcome-group PR touches main. Commander prose keeps using human task, intent, and outcome names rather than `bead`, bare namespace ids, or mannered filler. Before the first ISA claim closes on a consumer surface, run the verifier walker with `bun seats/walk.ts <claim-ref> --surface <kind>:<spec> --out .wheelhouse-runs/<task>/<surface-walk> --verifier verifier` and post the scrubbed walk transcript and verdict on the task before citing them in the claim (the local output is a git-excluded working copy, not something to commit), or write `Not walked:` on the claim.

Any script, hook or person hands this factory a signal with one command: `bun seats/intake.ts post --kind <goal|isa-change|report|error|finding> --source <name> --title <one line> --text <body>`. Each call adds one row to seats/intake.jsonl and does nothing else; the producer reads it from there.

```bash
bun seats/intake.ts post --kind <goal|isa-change|report|error|finding> --source <name> --title <one line> --text <body>
```

Replace the placeholders with values: choose one kind and quote multiword title/body arguments. The contract-writing skills live at `.agents/skills/intent/SKILL.md` and `.agents/skills/outcomes/SKILL.md`.

## Shutdown

The end-of-day ritual, in order (`wheelhouse/runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md`,
"End of day"): dispatch each active seat a write-your-handoff request — the
seat answers on its task record — then compose the fleet note at
`wheelhouse/HANDOFF.md` (per-seat status, waiting verdicts, tomorrow's first
actions), and only then:

```bash
bun seats/adapter.ts stop-all
```

Graceful, sessions kept; busy seats are reported and left running. A plain
machine shutdown loses nothing the graph holds — in-flight work resumes from
the graph, and `resume` brings a stopped seat back warm — but it skips the
handoff, and tomorrow's commander starts from raw state instead of a note.
